From 7e1e6d3a9922581c17e35e0cce05d10670e933fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 07:09:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] finish an elytra swap that has begun before the behaviour
 goes

The auto-swap's three window clicks are drained from ElytraBehavior#onTick0, so
the drain stops the moment ElytraProcess#onLostControl drops its reference to the
behaviour - not when destroy() runs, but at the assignment, and destroy() never
sees the transaction at all. A flight cancelled inside the one-to-
ticksBetweenInventoryMoves-tick gap between two of the three clicks therefore
left the queue simply never asked again: the spare elytra on the cursor and the
worn one still on the back, which is exactly the state the window rule in
InventoryTransaction exists to prevent, arrived at by a different road.

finishNow applies the rule the class already had for a window that goes away
mid-swap, with no "come back" to wait for. A sequence that has not begun is given
up whole and silently - nothing happened, so the job asks again. One that has
begun and whose window is still the player's own is finished now, remainder in
one go; the gaps tick keeps are a hand's timing and teardown is not a moment
anybody is watching. One that has begun whose window is not in front is left
exactly as it was and reported unfinished, because firing it would send clicks
the game drops and then clear a queue that never ran - turning "stalled and still
retryable" into "stalled and silently abandoned", which is strictly worse.

The ask sits in onLostControl and not in destroyBehaviorAsync on purpose. Every
caller of onLostControl is client-thread (PathingControlManager:59, :69 and
:208, plus ElytraProcess's own from onTick), so the click has a thread to be sent
on; destroyBehaviorAsync runs on the executor and is also reached by onWorldEvent
on the way out of a world, where there is nothing left to send it to. Putting it
in onLostControl excludes that path by construction rather than by a flag.

44 classes / 370 tests / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped, up from 44/364 at
e5c83449; InventoryTransactionTest goes 7 -> 13. Teeth proven by three
mutations: deleting the ask fails the source-level wiring pin; making finishNow
clear a begun queue and ignore the window fails exactly the two teardown tests
and leaves the other eleven green.

Two remainders are recorded in AGENTS.md's Known Problems rather than papered
over: a begun sequence with a container open cannot be finished and needs a drain
that outlives the behaviour, and trySwapElytra's guard reads its own fresh
transaction, so a cursor left dirty is invisible to it. The second is
pre-existing, inherited from before 31a1864c, and this change makes it rarer.
